{"parentUuid":"83828816-08b6-4a8d-928d-460beba540b0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ac213b46-6df0-41dc-b63c-ad9499c2a198","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dz1p82yzUibuw1X9Sahw7j","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now let me consolidate my findings in a comprehensive report. I have enough information to answer all 14 questions.\n\n## FINDINGS REPORT\n\nI've systematically explored the new `recap` feature and the dispatch infrastructure. Here are the facts, organized by your questions:\n\n---\n\n### A. The new `recap` feature\n\n**1-2. End-to-end flow, scope, and flags:**\n`entire recap` displays personal checkpoint activity (file: `cmd/entire/cli/recap.go`). It fetches from a cloud endpoint, not local-only. Flags:\n- `--day`, `--week`, `--month`, `--90` (mutually exclusive; default: today only) – **time scope only**, not per-repo filtering\n- `--agent <id>` (default: `all`) – filter to a specific agent\n- `--view [you|team|both]` (default: both) – which columns to render\n- `--color [auto|always|never]` (default: auto)\n- `--static` – skip interactive TUI and render plain text\n- `--insecure-http-auth` (hidden dev flag)\n\n**No `--repo` flag exists.** Instead, it detects current repo via git origin and passes it to the server (`cmd/entire/cli/recap.go:170-176`). The server narrows to that repo in the response.\n\n**3. User identification:**\n`recap` identifies the authenticated user via bearer token only. There is no git author email matching. The endpoint is `GET /api/v1/me/recap?since=...&until=...&repo=...` (`cmd/entire/cli/recap/me_recap.go:124`). The server-side token binding identifies \"me\" server-side (see `api/auth_tokens.go:14` — tokens carry a `user_id`). No client-side user filtering logic exists in the recap package.\n\n**4. Output format:**\nRecap renders **markdown-styled terminal text** (not HTML, not served static files). The `-static` flag triggers `RenderStaticRecap()` (`cmd/entire/cli/recap/render_static.go:29`) which produces colorized terminal box layouts. Interactive mode uses a TUI (`cmd/entire/cli/recap_tui.go`) that lets users toggle time range (t), view (v), and agent (a) with arrow keys.\n\nThe `static_server_test.go` filename was misleading — it tests serving the TUI with test fixtures, not static HTML generation.\n\n**5. HTTP endpoint:**\nRecap calls `GET /api/v1/me/recap` (`cmd/entire/cli/recap/me_recap.go:124`). **Different from dispatch**, which calls `POST /api/v1/dispatches/generate` with `CreateDispatchRequest`. No shared endpoint.\n\n---\n\n### B. Overlap with dispatch\n\n**6. Purpose overlap:**\nThese serve **different purposes**:\n- **`dispatch`** (2–7 day window by default): Generates a **polished markdown narrative** for *sharing with the team* about what was accomplished. Prose, voice customization, per-repo structure for communication. Designed for async updates to channels or status docs.\n- **`recap`** (today by default): Quick **personal activity dashboard** across agents/repos/skills for individual reflection. Quantitative metrics (sessions, checkpoints, tokens), tool-mix breakdown, contribution comparison (me vs team). Used for daily standup prep or personal productivity tracking.\n\nDispatch is \"I'm announcing my work\"; recap is \"what did I actually do today?\"\n\n**7. Design intent (from code comments):**\nNo CHANGELOG entry for recap yet (it's not in `CHANGELOG.md` at all), and git history shows only two commits: feat (#1015) and TUI (#1113). No explicit design doc in comments about dispatch-vs-recap philosophy. However, the name `me_recap.go` and endpoint path `/api/v1/me/recap` strongly suggest *personal use* vs dispatch's *team broadcast* intent.\n\n---\n\n### C. If we still want a `--me` flag on dispatch\n\n**8. Request parameter support:**\nThe `CreateDispatchRequest` struct (`cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/cloud.go:58-64`) has fields:\n```go\nRepos    []string `json:\"repos,omitempty\"`\nSince    string   `json:\"since\"`\nUntil    string   `json:\"until\"`\nGenerate bool     `json:\"generate\"`\nVoice    string   `json:\"voice,omitempty\"`\n```\n\n**No `author`, `user`, or filtering field exists.** Tests (`cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/cloud_test.go`) verify `repos`, `since`, `until`, `generate`, and `voice` are sent; they never check for or inject a user/author field. The request only carries time range and repo scope, not per-user scope.\n\nRecap's request (URL query string) also carries no user parameter; it's implicit in the bearer token. The dispatch endpoint almost certainly works the same way server-side—the bearer token identifies the user, and the request is about *which repos and time window*, not *which user's checkpoints*.\n\n**9. Bearer token identity:**\nYes, the token implicitly identifies the user server-side. All authenticated API calls use `api.Client` (`cmd/entire/cli/api/client.go:20-37`), which injects the bearer token in a custom transport (`bearerTransport.RoundTrip()`). The token carries a `user_id` server-side (`api/auth_tokens.go:14`). So a `--me` flag would be **a client-side boolean that asks the server \"filter to my own checkpoints only,\"** not a parameter you pass in the request body. The server would interpret it at request time by matching the token's user_id against checkpoint author metadata.\n\n**10. Checkpoint metadata and author info:**\nGit commits created during checkpoint recording embed author/committer as name+email (`cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/committed.go:1808`), using git config or fallback (`committed.go:1800`). However:\n- Dispatch tests don't show checkpoint HTTP payloads inspecting author email\n- No visible query filtering in `CreateDispatchRequest` or recap queries by author\n- Checkpoints are stored as git objects with signatures; the checkpoint metadata visible to the cloud API is likely *aggregated* (session counts, token counts) not per-commit author details\n\nThe cloud API likely infers authorship from the **uploader's bearer token** (who pushed the checkpoint), not by parsing git author email inside each checkpoint. This is why recap works: the server knows your token ↔ user_id mapping and can filter automatically.\n\n---\n\n### D. CLI plumbing patterns\n\n**11. Flag-to-request plumbing (example: `--voice`):**\nTrace for voice:\n1. Cobra flag defined (`dispatch.go:76`): `flagVoice`\n2. Passed to options resolver (`dispatch.go:52`): `parseDispatchFlags(..., flagVoice, ...)`\n3. Placed in `Options` struct (`dispatch/options.go:55`): `Voice: flagVoice`\n4. Read in `Run()` and included in `CreateDispatchRequest` (`dispatch/generate.go` or similar)\n5. HTTP POST body includes `Voice` field (`cloud.go:63`)\n\nTo add a new flag like `--me`:\n- Define cobra flag in `dispatch.go:22-31` (flagMe bool)\n- Add to `parseDispatchFlags()` call, update signature\n- Add to `ResolveOptions()` function and `Options` struct\n- Read it in the dispatcher that calls `CreateDispatch()` and conditionally set a field (or pass as URL query string if the server supports it)\n\n**12. Dispatch wizard (`dispatch_wizard.go`):**\nThe wizard is a form-based UI (`huh.Form`) that prompts for:\n- Mode (local vs server)\n- Time window\n- Branch selection (if local)\n- Repos (if server)\n- Voice\nIt's for users who run `entire dispatch` with no flags. A \"me/team\" toggle would fit naturally here as a choice field before \"confirm run.\" However, no such field currently exists in `dispatchWizardState` (`dispatch_wizard.go:55-65`).\n\n**13. Test patterns:**\nDispatch has robust test coverage:\n- `cloud_test.go`: Unit tests on the `CloudClient.CreateDispatch()` call, verifying request body fields (`TestCloudClient_CreateDispatch_Happy`, line 16; test verifies `repos` field in body)\n- `mode_cloud_test.go`: Integration-like tests on the full dispatch generation flow\n- `options_test.go`: Tests on flag parsing (e.g., `TestResolveOptions_NormalizesScopeValues`)\n\nTo add a `--me` flag test: add a test in `options_test.go` checking the flag rounds-trip through `ResolveOptions()`, and update `mode_cloud_test.go` to verify the request body includes the new field (if client-side) or verify server filtering (if server-side only).\n\n---\n\n### E. Naming consistency\n\n**14. Existing user-filter flags:**\nGrep across the entire CLI found **zero existing `--me`, `--mine`, `--author`, or `--user` flags** anywhere. The recap command **does use `--view [you|team|both]`** which is a perspective toggle, not a filter (both perspectives are always fetched server-side, only rendering changes). This naming (`you` vs `team`) is recap-specific and non-standard.\n\nFor dispatch, if a filter is added, the most consistent choice would be:\n- **`--me`** (aligns with `--view you` naming in recap)\n- **`--personal`** (clear intent, discoverable)\n\nBut there's **no existing CLI precedent** to follow.\n\n---\n\n### Summary for design synthesis\n\n- **Recap already does what a `--me dispatch` would do**, but on a shorter timescale and for personal reflection, not team communication.\n- **No shared endpoint or code path** between recap and dispatch.\n- **Adding `--me` to dispatch would require server-side support** (the endpoint doesn't currently accept a user filter parameter; the token implicitly identifies the user).\n- **Checkpoint authorship is tracked at push time** (via bearer token), not via git author email parsing.\n- **Test and flag plumbing patterns are established** for adding new fields to `CreateDispatchRequest`."}]}]},"uuid":"b143ece2-b0a3-4ce2-92b6-244b5f10fbce","timestamp":"2026-05-06T01:09:59.403Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I'm designing a way to let `entire dispatch` show \"just my work in a repo\" — today dispatch in cloud mode (`--repos owner/repo`) summarizes everyone's checkpoints, with no per-user filter. The user just pulled latest main and a new `cmd/entire/cli/recap/` package landed (with `me_recap.go`). I need to understand whether this new code already covers th